WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.360
Why is this oesophagus still healthy?

2
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.920
Researchers sequenced normal oesophagus from nine deceased organ donors.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:18.800
In middle-aged and elderly donors, NOTCH1-mutant cells made up an estimated 12 to

4
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.400
80% of the lining.

5
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.820
NOTCH1 mutations also turn up in oesophageal cancers.

6
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:31.180
Yet this tissue looked normal, and the mutations were more common here than in tumours.

7
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:37.040
The lining stays the same size only if cell gain matches cell loss.

8
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.960
In mouse oesophagus, only the basal progenitor cells divide.

9
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.140
Each division gives two progenitors, two differentiating daughters, or one of each.

10
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.540
In wild-type tissue, these three outcomes are balanced.

11
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.000
Differentiating cells move upward and are shed.

12
00:00:56.883 --> 00:01:00.403
A mutant clone wins by tilting that balance.

13
00:01:01.303 --> 00:01:05.903
Did the mutant cells simply divide faster, or did their daughters differentiate

14
00:01:05.903 --> 00:01:12.183
less? Notch signalling is linked to differentiation of squamous epithelial cells.

15
00:01:13.003 --> 00:01:18.723
In mouse oesophagus, Notch1-mutant clones expanded without a higher division rate.

16
00:01:19.863 --> 00:01:25.083
Tracking was consistent with more progenitor daughters and fewer differentiating ones.

17
00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:30.747
Count the progenitors one division makes: two times the chance of two progenitors,

18
00:01:31.247 --> 00:01:33.027
plus the chance of one of each.

19
00:01:34.187 --> 00:01:39.247
Balanced odds are 0.25, 0.5, and 0.25.

20
00:01:39.867 --> 00:01:42.567
That gives one, so the count holds steady.

21
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:49.407
Now tilt the odds to 0.35, 0.5, and 0.15.

22
00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:52.427
Then the average becomes 1.2.

23
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:58.850
Start with 100 progenitors and no cell death, in a simple model.

24
00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:05.150
Each generation multiplies the count by 1.2 with no change in cycle time.

25
00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:11.070
After three generations, you expect 172.8 progenitors.

26
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:16.830
The model shows that fate alone is enough, and the mouse tracking was consistent

27
00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:17.830
with that.

28
00:02:18.133 --> 00:02:22.133
A cancer gene mutation did not make this tissue malignant.

29
00:02:23.013 --> 00:02:28.493
In mouse oesophagus, wild-type neighbours at the clone edge differentiated faster.

30
00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:34.653
In a mouse model, early lesions were removed more often when the surrounding clones

31
00:02:34.653 --> 00:02:35.913
were highly competitive.

32
00:02:36.993 --> 00:02:40.273
That is a mouse result, not human prevention data.

33
00:02:40.273 --> 00:02:47.153
So ask which clones expand, and why, not only which mutations a tissue carries.
